[PASS] S1.exclusion.threshold.20   |  the specified threshold |A| >= 20 admits 0 of the 19900 targets of sample A (max |A| = 11): the exclusion removes 100% of a natural exact sample

  WHY, AND IT IS NOT A SAMPLING ARTEFACT.  By the rigidity lemma,
  |A(x)| >= k forces max A(x) >= s_k, where s_1 = 2 and
  s_{i+1} = s_i(s_i - 1) + 1 (Sylvester's sequence).  Hence
     |A| >= 20  ==>  max A >= s_20 , a number with 106721 decimal digits
     (s_6 = 3263443, s_7 = 10650056950807 has 14 digits; s_8 has 27 digits).
  Computing lambda at such an element means factoring a 106721-digit
  integer.  The class of targets the specification asks SS2 to use is
  therefore EMPTY of objects whose parity class exists for us.
[PASS] S1.sylvester.ceiling   |  |A(x)| >= 20 implies max A(x) >= s_20 > 10^106720, so no admissible target can be constructed, let alone factored: the domain of the specified SS2 test is provably empty
[PASS] S1.empty.computable.domain   |  of the 20300 computed supports, 0 have |A| >= 20 (so the size class is non-empty as a rational object); 0 of those have max A <= 1e13, so the number of targets with an admissible support AND a computable parity class is 0

  WHY SMALL SUPPORTS ARE DEGENERATE (the trap that was already hit):
  for |A| = 1 the support is {m_1} and any 'agreement statistic' with
  lambda is literally the single value lambda(m_1) = +/-1 -- a coin
  flip, no statistic.  For |A| = k the size-matched random set in the
  same range has sign-mean standard deviation ~ 1/sqrt(k), i.e. 0.71
  at k = 2 and 0.50 at k = 4; a per-target 'agreement' at these sizes
  is pure noise, and the pooled statistic is dominated by the one or
  two positions that nearly every such support shares (SS2 measures
  this).  Sample A has 898 supports of size 1 and 10465 of size <= 3.
[PASS] S1.small.supports.degenerate   |  898 of 19900 sample-A targets have a single-element support, where the 'parity statistic' degenerates to lambda of one integer

