==============================================================================
SS4  Bridge B3: f(n) = lambda(n)lambda(n+2) is not multiplicative and
     D(s) = sum f(n) n^-s has no Euler product
==============================================================================

  f(1) = lambda(1)lambda(3) = -1.  Multiplicativity requires f(1)=1,
  so the failure is immediate; the coprime pairs below make it concrete.
    f(2)f(11) = (-1)(+1) = -1   vs   f(22) = +1   DIFFER
    f(2)f(13) = (-1)(-1) = +1   vs   f(26) = -1   DIFFER
    f(2)f(17) = (-1)(+1) = -1   vs   f(34) = +1   DIFFER
    f(3)f(5) = (+1)(+1) = +1   vs   f(15) = -1   DIFFER
[PASS] S4.not.multiplicative   |  f(1) = -1 != 1; f(2)f(11) = -1 != f(22) = +1; f(2)f(13) = +1 != f(26) = -1; 12016 coprime pairs a,b <= 200 with ab <= 1e6 violate multiplicativity (smallest: a=3, b=4, ab=12)

  The naive multiplicative model: if f were multiplicative then
  D(2) = prod_p (1 + sum_k f(p^k) p^{-2k}) exactly.  At s = 2 the
  series converges absolutely, so every truncation is bounded:
    D(2)  = sum_{n<=1000000} f(n)/n^2            = -1.1268130386
    prod_{p<=100000, p^k<=1000000} (...)            = 0.8875735163
    |D(2) - product|                       = 2.0143865549
    n-tail   |sum_{n>N} f(n)n^-2| <= 2/N   = 2.0000e-06
    p-tail   <= zeta(2)(e^{1/P}-1)         = 1.6449e-05
    k-tail   (inner sums truncated at N)   <= 1.0000e-09
    rigorous total tail bound              = 1.8450e-05
    rh46's looser product bound            = 1.80898647
  The discrepancy exceeds the rigorous tail by a factor 109178.
[PASS] S4.no.euler.product   |  |D(2) - naive Euler product| = 2.014387, which exceeds the rigorous truncation bound 1.845042e-05 by a factor 109178 (rh46 reported the same discrepancy 2.01440934 against its looser bound): f has no Euler product, so no zeta/Perron route reaches the correlation

  VERDICT B3: no Euler product; the correlation is not multiplicative
  and the zeta-based machinery does not apply to it.
